    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase.\n\n- The three new exception classes land in `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.util` alongside the existing domain exceptions (`DisposableEmailException`, `InvalidAddressException`, `InvalidPostcodeException`, etc.), matching the established convention exactly.\n- Each new `@ExceptionHandler` method in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` mirrors the existing handlers' shape precisely (debug log \u2192 status \u2192 `detailBuild(...)` \u2192 `ResponseEntity.status(status).body(detail)`), so the class stays a consistent catalog of per-exception translators rather than becoming a catch-all.\n- Responsibilities are in the right units: the controller makes the rejection decision and throws a domain exception; the advice owns HTTP-status + problem-body translation. Replacing the bare `new ResponseEntity<>(status)` returns with thrown exceptions is the correct move to route 409/429 through the same RFC7807 path the spec requires, and it removes the two code paths that previously bypassed the problem-body builder.\n- The small, deliberate variation of passing `e.getMessage()` as the detail (vs. the shared `ERROR_INVALID_REQUEST` constant used by the 400 handlers) is reasonable since these new exceptions carry meaningful, user-facing messages, and it is applied consistently across all three new handlers.\n\nNo duplication beyond the per-handler boilerplate that is already the file's intentional pattern, and a maintainer here would find this entirely natural.",
